Subject: [PATCH] MPEG: the picture charge in the frames time scale

sceMpegGetPicture (guest-buffered mode) charges a fixed 10 ms of EE cycles per picture.
That time is the console's: charge it scaled like the game's own code (the cycle scale)
while the VBlanks run at a PC rate; unchanged at the console's VBlanks. PS2X_MPEG_SCALE_CHARGE=0: as before.
(Transformers TODO 113: the HQ level previews held the HQ to 80 frames a second.)
---
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h
index c8e9e29..9466ad0 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ee_scheduler.h
@@ -386,6 +386,12 @@ public:
     // A factor for the cycles executed guest code is charged (0: PS2X_EE_CYCLE_SCALE, 1 by
     // default). Below 1 the EE runs faster than the console's: more code fits in a VBlank.
     void setCycleScale(double factor) noexcept;
+    // The factor executed guest code is charged now, in 1/256 (setCycleScale's, else PS2X_EE_CYCLE_SCALE's; 256 = 1).
+    [[nodiscard]] uint32_t currentCycleScale256() const noexcept
+    {
+        const uint32_t override = m_cycleScaleOverride.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+        return override ? override : m_defaultScale256;
+    }
     // The VBlank period (0: the console's, kConsoleVBlankPeriod). It takes effect from the
     // VBlank after the next one is scheduled; the game's frames follow the VBlanks, so a game
     // that simulates a frame per VBlank runs at this rate (a PC port's frame rate).
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
index c9dfcc1..cb96b09 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/MPEG.cpp
@@ -2930,7 +2930,29 @@ namespace ps2_stubs
                 // block into the same buffer, and the driver (which looks every 5 ms)
                 // must have taken the first one by then.
                 constexpr uint32_t kPictureDecodeCycles = 2'949'120u; // 10 ms
-                runtime->eeScheduler().accountCycles(kPictureDecodeCycles);
+                // That time is the console's, so it is charged in the time scale the game's frames run at, as
+                // the game's own code is (EeScheduler::currentCycleScale256): a port running the game at R
+                // frames a second scales its code by 30/R and its VBlanks by 60/R, and a fixed 10 ms then spilled
+                // every frame that took a picture into the next VBlank (a game at 240 has 4.2 ms a frame) - the
+                // HQ's level previews held its frames to 80 a second. While the VBlanks are the console's (a
+                // movie of the window's own, the boot) the scale stays 1: the charge as before.
+                // PS2X_MPEG_SCALE_CHARGE=0: always the fixed 10 ms.
+                static const bool scaleCharge = []()
+                {
+                    const char *env = std::getenv("PS2X_MPEG_SCALE_CHARGE");
+                    return !(env && *env == '0');
+                }();
+                EeScheduler &ee = runtime->eeScheduler();
+                uint32_t charge = kPictureDecodeCycles;
+                if (scaleCharge && ee.vblankPeriod() != EeScheduler::kConsoleVBlankPeriod)
+                    charge = static_cast<uint32_t>((static_cast<uint64_t>(kPictureDecodeCycles) * ee.currentCycleScale256()) >> 8u);
+                static uint32_t lastSaid = kPictureDecodeCycles;
+                if (charge != lastSaid)
+                {
+                    lastSaid = charge;
+                    std::cerr << "[MPEG] a picture's decoding charged " << charge / 294912.0 << " ms of EE time (the frames' time scale)" << std::endl;
+                }
+                ee.accountCycles(charge);
             }
         }
         else if (frameCount == 0u)
